host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title Caravan relay -- a chain crosses two seams without waiting, and the far end is taken on the relay's word
prompt Autonomous loop: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Microkernel Target double-seat, Standfast on reds first, send each finished increment.
think Standfast first. Read crux/REDS.md whole: rows 80, 83, 84, 85 and the prose notes 81, 85-reopened, 86, 87 all read Closed or Released, and the last three laps each opened new work beside them. Row 80's booked follow-on -- a count guard that computes its own count -- was closed by REDS #81's registration sweep and the guard now embeds in the suite. No open agent-closable red stands, so the line runs.
think Council rota, lap 458 of the 27-doc d27 -- item 26, active-designing/20260812-171050_the-1024-round-itinerary.md. Its filling law is the one this lap leans on: a quest is filled, never invented, and reds preempt across any boundary. The itinerary also names why this ring matters past Caravan -- Season 1 Journey 2's publish rung reaches a Comlink-served custody gate, and a served chain is what stands underneath it.
think The last lap's recommend named many-to-many with a possible cycle. Reading stall.rye and serial_two_clients.bron showed the honest ordering: before a cycle can exist, one domain has to hold two roles at once, and no declaration in this arc has ever seated one. So the chain comes first and the cycle follows -- the relay is the prerequisite the cycle is built on, not a smaller version of it.
think A new declaration was needed for the first time in five rings. serial_relay.bron seats client -- relay_virt -- backend across two channels and five directed regions: ask_up, ask_down, answer_down, answer_up, and taken. Same domain and channel counts as serial_two_clients; a different shape entirely. The counts cannot tell you which; the grants can.
think That observation became the ring's first check. A region granted rw to exactly one domain and r to exactly one other is a one-way street, so the whole flow graph derives from the grant table alone -- 5 flows out of 10 grants -- and the single domain spanning two peers is the relay. No role word is written anywhere in the declaration. The shape is arithmetic the document already carries.
think taken earned its place as the fifth region rather than a second index inside another. queue.rye puts one index at offset zero of each region, so a region carries exactly one number; the client's own tail therefore wants a region the client alone may write. That is the stall ring's law carried one hop further -- the side that bears a loss is the only side that can report it drained.
think The deadlock question answered itself once the plan was drawn. A client's ask has no answer on the pass it arrives, since the answer lives two hops away, so a relay that waited would wait forever on the first pass. Every verb is a bounded pass that moves what stands and returns. To prove it rather than assert it, the plan asks a second pair while nothing at all stands answered.
think The isolation question turned out to be the finding worth carrying. client and backend share no channel and no region, which is exactly what keeps two hops from collapsing into one -- and it leaves the client nothing to attest the far end with. So claimed_author checks the sequence and the origin, which the client can know for itself, and reports the author as a claim. Memory attests a neighbor; it cannot attest a stranger.
think Wrote HopList with its own max_hops of 6 rather than bending fanin.NameList's 4, since the settling step reads the whole chain at once. Compose, never braid -- the same call stall.rye made when PolicyList stood beside NameList rather than inside it.
think Held each step to its verb's own arity in read_plan. A forwarding pass arriving with a collector's two regions would otherwise read its backlog out of whichever name sat at the third index, so a shape that disagrees with its verb is refused rather than run.
obs GREEN on the first metal pass, before either probe. The two RED probes were then run to earn the green rather than assume it.
obs The blocking probe was decisive. Making the relay refuse to forward while any ask stood unanswered stopped the second forwarding pass dead at QueueFull, with two asks in hand and the whole chain frozen behind it -- the exact failure a chain exists to rule out, and it cost four lines to produce.
obs The isolation probe went RED one layer earlier than expected, and better for it. Granting the backend a second reader on answer_up broke the one-writer-one-reader shape and the flow derivation refused the declaration outright. Opening a proper back channel instead -- a fresh region rw to backend and r to client, every region still directed -- made three domains span two peers, and the shape check refused a chain with no single middle. The derivation is a real check rather than decoration.
obs Caught a fabricated stamp before send. The version constant was first written as 20260819.171204, a time never read from any clock; TZ=America/New_York date gave 20260819.162139 and the constant was corrected. Live-clock stamps are read, never invented ([[live-clock-stamps]]).
obs No new outcome code was needed. Every way this ring ends already had a word in the arc's vocabulary -- carried, NotGranted, WriteDenied, NothingNew, bytes differed -- which is a small sign the chain is the same system one hop longer rather than a new one.
loom witness=caravan_relay_witness asks=4 hops=5 flows=5 grants=10 passes=8 walls=2 bells=4 rings=19
loom red_probe=blocking_relay result=second_forward_refused_QueueFull outcome=chain_frozen_with_two_asks_in_hand
loom red_probe=back_channel_between_ends result=three_domains_span_two_peers outcome=chain_has_no_single_middle
file caravan/systems/serial_relay.bron the first declaration in the arc with a middle -- three domains, two channels, five directed regions, ten grants
file caravan/relay.rye the ring itself -- the shape derived from grants, a pass that never waits, and a far end named as a claim
file tools/caravan_relay_witness.rish the witness -- GREEN on metal, every claim read natively with contains
file caravan/README.md the ladder gains its nineteenth row and the prose that says why memory attests a neighbor and not a stranger
file crux/REMEMBER.md the live edge moves to relay; the stall edge condenses into the prior-edges line
recommend keep-going -- next the cycle: two relays wired to each other, where a domain's downstream is also its upstream and the flow graph closes on itself for the first time. The derivation already computes the graph, so a cycle is a fact it can name; the crux is whether a closed flow graph with no waiting verb still settles, and what a pass owes a peer that is asking it back. No key, no funds, no gate.
